The module layer requests a redraw of the module's view after feeding it (src/module/module.c:833-834); the shell has no scheduler handle and requests none for the model's own view, so a change in the model view does not make the shell draw again. The view's own API says when it needs a new frame (canlin_ui_view_pending, canlin_ui_view_dirty) and the shell exposes the new rectangle as CANLIN_UI_P_MODEL_VIEW. Decide who asks: the app (which owns the model view and the scheduler) or the shell (which would need a scheduler handle). Found by the 2026-11-14 work on k128.

Done in the platform lane: the app is the layer that asks. canlin_app_assistant_update (src/app/assistant.c:456) asks the assistant module to draw again (canlin_ev_invalidate, ask_frame at src/app/assistant.c:447) when the model was replaced (the assistant view's revision changed) or when the model's own view has queued input that only a frame can apply. The module's draw produces the frame of the model's own view (the binding draws the model into it - k130) and the shell draws that new frame (model_new_frame, src/shell/shell.c). The shell needs no scheduler handle: the app owns the view and the scheduler. The queued input is only read when the view has a frame - a view nobody draws into keeps its queued input for ever and asking again and again would keep the scheduler busy (until k130 draws the model into that view). Test: t/app/test_assistant.c test_model_redraw (line 505) - proved red without the request (2 checks failed: the frame count and the model's button in the record). Side effect: the new model is on screen right after the answer instead of at the next input.

